  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Thursday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Skies are mostly clear this morning\nacross northern and central Nevada. Current weather pattern is\nshowing a large expansive ridge across Arizona and New Mexico \nwith an upper trough over the Pacific Northwest states. For today,\na southwesterly flow continues over the forecast area, allowing \nlimited mid-level moisture to move into portions of central and \nnorthern Nevada. This will continue the trend in afternoon \nconvection across the area. Looking for storms to be more on the \ndry side. Antecedent drought conditions and dried out fuels are \nmitigating the effects of any precipitation associated with \nconvection this week. As a result, lightning strikes have been \nsuccessful in igniting numerous fires across the area. \nThunderstorms today will continue this trend, leading to possible \nnew fire starts. Highs today will be in the 90s with lows in the \n50s. Winds will be breezy with gusts of 20 to 25 mph during the \nafternoon.\n\nThe area receives a double shot of moisture on Friday and into the\nweekend as residual moisture from a tropical system off the CA \nCoast will filter into western Nevada. Precipitable water values \nare forecast to be anomalously high (1+ to 1.2 inches) on Friday.\nThis has resulted in a marginal risk of excessive rainfall over \nthe area according to the Weather Prediction Center. Rainfall \namounts Friday and Friday night will range 0.25-0.5 inches across \nthe forecast area. Said rainfall and resulting cloud cover will \nlead to a cooling trend into the weekend that will send \ntemperatures a few degrees below normal for late August. Cooler\nhigh temperatures are expected during the afternoon with readings\nin the 80s with overnight lows in the 50s.\n\nSaturday is a bit concerning with respect to active weather and\nstrong storms. The atmosphere is expected to remain rather moist \nwith precipitable water values at or above 0.75 inches. Lapse \nrates are forecast to steepen with lifted indices forecast to be \naround -2C to -4C or so, and will be coincident with 0-3km bulk \nshear values of 30-40 knots. DCAPE values in central Nevada will \nbe at or above 1000K/kg, which could lead to outflow wind gusts \ngreater than 58 mph. Will need to monitor the ongoing situation.\nCooling continues across the forecast area with highs in the 70s\nand 80s. Lows will dip into the 40s and 50s.\n\nBeyond the weekend the 4C/ULT hybrid setup looks to break down \nresulting in the potential for quieter weather as we head into \nSeptember. \n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence continues for\nafternoon thunderstorm activity over the area today. Confidence \nis moderate regarding increased precipitation Friday and Saturday\nas well as an associated cooling trend. Adjustments were made to\nWx, PoP grids in the short term to highlight thunderstorm\ndevelopment.",
